    "result_text": "I implemented `summarize_ticket` in `app/summary.py`, and all 4 tests pass, including 2 new ones.\n\n```python\nmessages = [SystemMessage(SUMMARY_PROMPT), HumanMessage(ticket_text(ticket))]\nreply = chat.invoke(messages, stop=[\"\\n\\n\"])\nreturn str(reply.text).strip()\n```\n\n- **One request:** it makes a single `chat.invoke` call with the system prompt and the ticket as the user message, and passes `stop=[\"\\n\\n\"]`.\n- **Plain string reply:** I used `reply.text` rather than `str(reply.content)`. Some providers return the reply as a list of blocks instead of a string, and `str()` on that list would give you its Python text form instead of the reply. `.text` pulls out just the text parts. Wrapping it in `str()` makes the result a plain `str`, since `.text` returns a subclass of `str`.\n\n**Tests:** I added `tests/test_summary.py`. It uses a small fake chat model that records each call. The tests check that there is exactly one call, the message types and contents, the stop sequence, whitespace stripping, and replies that come back as a list of blocks.\n\n**Docs lookup:** CLAUDE.md asks for API lookups through Context7, but its monthly quota was used up. Instead, I checked `BaseChatModel.invoke(..., stop=...)` and `BaseMessage.text` in the installed langchain-core 1.6.5 source. No dependencies were added."
